          { name: "Economic Development & Job Quality in Black & Hispanic Neighborhoods", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.48% Black and 25.58% Hispanic populations together represent two-thirds of the district, yet there is no evidence in the election result or opposition activity of either community being mobilized around specific economic development priorities. The $81,970 median income and 31.7% college attainment suggest working-class constituencies with limited access to wealth-building assets and professional opportunity pathways. A candidate — incumbent or challenger — who articulates a specific plan for neighborhood-level economic development, small business support, and workforce development in majority-Black and majority-Hispanic areas can build durable constituent relationships that a generic Democratic or Republican posture cannot." },
          { name: "Responsive Community Safety & Police Accountability", tag: "careful", why: "The 40.48% Black constituency has decades of experience with police accountability failures and extractive relationships with law enforcement systems. While this is traditionally Democratic messaging terrain, a Republican challenger could credibly position themselves as more responsive to community safety concerns — including police accountability, community oversight, and responsive local governance — if the Democratic incumbent is perceived as dismissive of these concerns or overly deferential to police institutions. This is a constituency for whom responsive governance means accountability, not an ideological position on criminal justice." },
          { name: "Positioning the Unopposed Result as Evidence of Incumbent Accountability", tag: "avoid", why: "The Democratic incumbent's unopposed status should not be framed as evidence of constituent satisfaction or incumbent responsiveness; it is more likely evidence of a lack of competitive pressure and Republican underinvestment in this district. Any incumbent who claims the unopposed victory as validation of their constituent service is misreading a structural fact — the absence of opposition does not prove effectiveness, it proves the absence of a challenger. This framing is vulnerable to any competent Republican campaign that emphasizes unaddressed constituent needs." },
          { name: "Running on Democratic Party Loyalty Rather Than Material Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "With a 40% Black majority, a 25.58% Hispanic population, and a 27.4% renter base, the Democratic incumbent holds this seat through default demographic alignment, not earned constituent trust. Campaigning on party loyalty, abstract progressive values, or inherited Democratic margins is exactly the posture that makes this seat vulnerable to a Republican challenger who enters with concrete promises on housing affordability, neighborhood economic development, and responsive local governance. The unopposed status has created no electoral pressure to deliver on material results; any challenger who creates that pressure can immediately make this district competitive." }
