          { name: "Public Education & Educational Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 30.5% college attainment (below state average) and a 39.0 median age reflecting a working-class family-oriented constituency, public education quality and educational equity are primary concerns for households with school-age children. Chicago's South Side has historically faced disinvestment in school resources, teacher quality, and educational infrastructure — issues that directly affect family economic mobility and housing decisions (school catchment areas drive where working-class families can afford to live). A representative who engages education funding, teacher compensation, school facility investment, and equitable resource distribution with specificity is addressing the single most direct lever for intergenerational economic mobility that the state controls." },
          { name: "Distinct Black and Latino Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 48.09% Black and 17.84% Latino populations together represent 65.9% of the district, but these communities have distinct economic profiles, healthcare priorities (maternal health, asthma prevalence, chronic disease), and educational concerns that can become obscured in a generic 'majority community' framework. Latino voters in IL-HD-8 have specific concerns around immigration policy, workplace protections, and linguistic access to services; Black voters have specific concerns around criminal justice, economic development, and institutional racism in public services. A representative who treats these constituencies as separate groups requiring distinct policy responsiveness and constituent attention builds deeper community relationships than one who merges them into an abstract majority." },
          { name: "Taking the 75% Margin for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "A 75% Democratic margin is substantial but not immovable — it reflects active voter alignment with the representative's demonstrated commitment to working-class economic improvement, not inherited Democratic loyalty. In working-class constituencies with high renter rates and constrained incomes, erosion of turnout and vote share can occur rapidly if voters perceive that their representative has stopped actively fighting for their material interests. The 2020 experience of Democratic underperformance in urban working-class districts demonstrates that even strong baseline partisanship can degrade when constituents feel neglected or perceive that the representative prioritizes abstract principle over concrete economic delivery." },
          { name: "Framing the District as Post-Racial or Merger of Distinct Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "A 48% Black, 17.84% Latino, 30.45% white district with deep historical segregation, economic inequality, and institutional racism present in housing, employment, education, and criminal justice is not a 'diverse' district that has transcended race — it is a district where racial inequality is the structural reality shaping economic opportunity and access to resources. Any representative who frames the district as if racial categories are merely demographic descriptors rather than indicators of distinct institutional experiences, barriers, and policy needs is failing to engage the constituency's actual lived reality. Specificity about how policy addresses racial inequality and institutional barriers is the foundation of credible engagement with Black and Latino voters in IL-HD-8." }
