          { name: "Property Tax Sustainability and Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 79.4% homeownership in a district where median income is $126,137, property tax fairness and whether tax burdens are rising faster than property values or income is a material governance concern. High-income homeowners in well-maintained districts are highly attentive to tax policy because they have the capacity to relocate; a representative who fails to manage the property tax issue will find 69% margins eroding quickly among voters who can afford to move." },
          { name: "Asian Community Engagement and Representation", tag: "careful", why: "The 18.73% Asian population is substantially above state and national averages and reflects families oriented toward professional advancement, educational achievement, and family economic security. At this percentage, the Asian community is large enough to be a meaningful constituency; understanding and responding to community-specific priorities around school equity, professional pathways, and economic opportunity matters to whether this population sees itself represented. Treating this as a generic minority constituency rather than engaging its specific priorities risks inadvertently signaling that representation is not genuine." },
          { name: "Taking 69% as Permission to Govern Complacently", tag: "avoid", why: "A 69% result in a highly educated, high-income constituency that evaluates on outcomes is not a mandate to govern less carefully; it is evidence of alignment between the incumbent's record and constituent expectations. Any decline in school performance, any perception of municipal service degradation, or any sense that the district's quality-of-life infrastructure is deteriorating will cause margins to erode among voters sophisticated enough to recognize governance failure and wealthy enough to live elsewhere." },
          { name: "Assuming Housing Affordability is Not a Constituency Concern", tag: "avoid", why: "While this is a high-income district, the 21.6% renter rate includes younger professional families attempting to establish themselves and form households — and these voters may be supporting Democrats partly because they perceive the incumbent as responsive to affordability challenges. A representative who ignores or dismisses housing cost concerns risks alienating the younger, more diverse portion of the coalition that keeps margins sustainable against Republican challenge in suburban terrain." }
