          { name: "Treating 78% as a Permanent Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 78-22 partisan margin can create false confidence that the seat is safe and does not require active constituent engagement on material economic concerns; in reality, districts with 28.6% renters and working-to-middle-class income are highly responsive to candidate performance on housing, jobs, and economic security. A Democratic candidate who assumes the 78% baseline is permanent and neglects concrete economic delivery risks erosion toward 65-70% if opposition effectively mobilizes around unmet economic needs." },
          { name: "Assuming Black Voter Loyalty Without Addressing Material Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "The 49.78% Black majority and 71.63% combined non-white population create a strong Democratic demographic base, but this should not translate into assuming Black voter engagement without a concrete legislative record on affordable housing, business investment, workforce development, and economic opportunity in predominantly Black neighborhoods. Voters evaluate on material delivery, not symbolic representation, and Democratic candidates who take Black community economic concerns for granted risk lower turnout or candidate switching if opposition offers specific policy alternatives." }
