          { name: "Hispanic Community Growth & Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 22.11% of the district and growing, the Hispanic community is HD-33's second-largest demographic group, yet represents only about one-third the size of the Black majority. Language access, immigrant family economic integration, and workplace protections are constituent needs that require specific legislative attention and cultural competency from the representative. A legislator who engages the Black majority but treats the Hispanic community as peripheral or secondary is underserving roughly one-fifth of the district and missing an opportunity to build durable cross-community coalition that strengthens the seat against any future challenge." },
          { name: "Renter Majority & Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 42.8% renter rate in a district with $55,947 median income and 62.89% Black population reflects a constituency with limited housing wealth and high exposure to displacement through rent increases, predatory lending, and gentrification pressure. Housing policy — rent stabilization, tenant protections, community land trusts, down-payment assistance programs — are direct economic security issues for a plurality of households, yet are often treated as secondary to broader economic messaging. An incumbent who speaks to renters' specific constraints and pushes for concrete housing policy remedies strengthens the seat; one who defaults to abstract economic growth messaging risks the perception that government is not addressing the lived concerns of working families." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed Result as Permanent Electoral Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed Democratic win reflects current alignment, not electoral certainty — particularly in a district where 42.8% of households are renters at median income and 23.6% hold college degrees. If constituent frustration accumulates around housing affordability, school quality, or perceived lack of responsiveness to economic concerns, a primary challenge from a more aggressive alternative is a realistic possibility. Incumbents who run 2020 campaigns in 2024 or assume that partisan alignment alone sustains a seat are arithmetically underestimating the volatility that economic anxiety creates in working-class constituencies." },
          { name: "Framing Renter Households as Secondary to Homeowner Economic Interests", tag: "avoid", why: "With 42.8% renters, HD-33 has nearly as many renter households as homeowners, yet much state-level economic policy (property tax relief, down-payment assistance, tax-advantaged savings accounts) privileges homeownership. Any legislator who unconsciously defaults to homeowner framing or treats renter households as temporary or less-rooted members of the community will alienate a plurality of constituents whose economic security and long-term housing stability are equally urgent. This is particularly consequential in a majority-Black district where historical exclusion from homeownership wealth-building has created a renter-dominant demographic profile that requires affirmative policy attention, not default dismissal." }
