          { name: "Treating 81% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% result in a district with 57.7% renters, 52.75% Hispanic composition, and 34.4-year median age is a high water mark reflecting strong cultural affinity and low opposition investment, not a stable floor. This constituency is acutely sensitive to economic conditions and material service delivery; if the incumbent is perceived as not delivering on housing, family economic security, or neighborhood investment, that 81% can erode quickly to a primary or independent challenger. Treating 81% as sufficient or sustainable is the most dangerous strategic error an incumbent can make in IL-HD-39." },
          { name: "Assuming Passive Constituent Loyalty Based on Partisan Alignment", tag: "avoid", why: "Young renter families and Hispanic working-class voters do not default to partisan loyalty in the absence of visible, concrete governance delivery. The 81% reflects approval of the incumbent's performance, not immunity from challenge if that performance is perceived as insufficient. An incumbent who becomes passive about constituent relationship, assumes base turnout without active engagement, or neglects the material economic concerns that drive daily decision-making in renter households will find that 81% vulnerable to a challenger who offers more credible commitment to housing, family economic security, and neighborhood investment. The margin is real but conditional on active, continuous constituent service." }
