          { name: "Treating the 64% as a Safe Majority Rather Than a Delivered Margin", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 result in a working-class district where half the population is Hispanic and a third are renters is held by active incumbent delivery on constituent concerns, not by inherited Democratic loyalty. Hispanic voters at the $81K income level respond to economic performance and governance competence; if the incumbent becomes disconnected from the community, fails to deliver on housing or school quality, or appears to take the margin for granted, opposition mobilization has a real opening. The margin is large enough to withstand routine challenges but fragile enough to collapse if the incumbent stops actively demonstrating that government is working for working families' economic security." },
          { name: "Polarized Partisan Rhetoric Over Concrete Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where nearly 60% of residents are Hispanic, where median income is solid but not affluent, and where younger households are managing tight budgets, abstract partisan debate about ideology or national culture war issues will appear disconnected from the material concerns that shape daily life. Hispanic working families and renters are asking whether their representative is fighting for their economic security, housing stability, and children's opportunity — not whether they have the 'correct' positions on national partisan dividing lines. An incumbent who defaults to national partisan messaging rather than rooting legislative work in local constituent concerns risks appearing tone-deaf to the community they represent, which will erode the margin among swing voters who are pragmatically evaluating whether this representative delivers for them." }
