          { name: "Education Funding and School Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 59% college attainment and median age 41.1, IL-HD-47 is disproportionately populated by parents and professionals with children or expectations of strong public schools. These constituents evaluate school systems with specificity—teacher compensation, curriculum design, academic outcomes—and will change their vote if a candidate articulates a credible plan for sustainable education investment that does not rely on property tax increases alone. The 50-50 split suggests neither candidate has yet made a compelling case on this dimension." },
          { name: "Property Tax Sustainability and Fiscal Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "Affluent suburban households at $124,462 income face a specific tax vulnerability: rising property assessments in high-demand areas create escalating tax burdens even as incomes stagnate. This constituency is not anti-tax in principle—they support public investment—but they demand evidence that tax dollars are managed efficiently and that rate increases correlate with measurable service improvements. A candidate who speaks to the specific mechanics of tax policy, reassessment reform, and the relationship between revenue and outcomes will move persuadable voters in this education-intensive market." },
          { name: "Understanding the 50-50 Electorate as a Sign of Policy Failure", tag: "careful", why: "The perfect partisan split is not a sign that the district is genuinely divided between two coherent political visions—it is a sign that both candidates failed to build confidence on the material concerns of a college-educated, affluent suburban constituency. Any candidate who assumes the 50% they received represents a stable floor rather than a contingent endorsement pending performance on education, tax policy, and healthcare will find that support eroding when a more specific competitor emerges. This constituency is actively evaluating, not passively loyal." },
          { name: "Partisan Base Mobilization Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "A traditional partisan turnout strategy—mobilizing core supporters through ideological appeals or national political narratives—will not move the needle in a district that is 59% college-educated, $124K+ median income, and locked at 50-50. The voters who remain persuadable are those evaluating on local governance competence, fiscal management, and specific policy outcomes, not those motivated by abstract partisan identity. Spending resources on base mobilization in HD-47 is spending them in the wrong market." },
          { name: "National Culture War Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban professionals earning six figures are not the constituency most animated by national culture-war narratives—they are more concerned with practical governance and whether public investment delivers measurable outcomes. A candidate who leads on divisive national social issues rather than education quality, fiscal transparency, and healthcare access is misreading the district's actual swing constituency and ceding persuadable voters to a more locally-focused opponent." }
