          { name: "Fiscal Competence & Property Tax Stewardship", tag: "lean-into", why: "With $106,778 median household income and 78.5% homeownership, IL-HD-48 is a property-holding constituency for whom tax burden and municipal fiscal management are direct material concerns. College-educated homeowners evaluate on whether the representative demonstrates concrete knowledge of property assessment, school funding mechanisms, and municipal budget prioritization — not abstract conservative positioning. A candidate who speaks with specificity about property tax fairness, school district efficiency, and municipal accountability commands credibility that generic Republican messaging does not." },
          { name: "Educated Homeowner Governance Evaluation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 41% college attainment rate and 42% Democratic opposition in an affluent suburban district indicates a constituency sensitive to competence, responsiveness, and policy detail rather than inherited partisanship. These voters evaluate candidates on whether they demonstrate visible problem-solving capacity, constituent service engagement, and fiscal transparency — the material governance concerns of educated professionals managing household finances and property assets. A campaign that leads with demonstrable governance performance rather than partisan narrative speaks the language this constituency actually uses to decide." },
          { name: "The 42% Opposition as a Swing Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "In an affluent, college-educated suburban district, the 42% Democratic opposition includes significant numbers of educated homeowners evaluating on governance and fiscal stewardship rather than partisan identity. These are swing voters, not a fixed Democratic base. Any candidate who assumes the opposition is ideologically determined and therefore unreachable, rather than treating them as voters whose concerns about local accountability and municipal performance are addressable, is leaving competitive terrain unclaimed. The margin between 58% and a loss is small enough that movement among educated homeowners on governance concerns is electorally consequential." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging as a Primary Campaign Framework", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where college-educated homeowners dominate and the median income is $106,778, partisan messaging about state or national ideological issues has less persuasive power than demonstrated competence on local governance. A campaign that relies primarily on Republican messaging about taxes or government size, without grounding those claims in specific knowledge of how IL-HD-48's municipalities are actually managed, reads as inattentive to the specific constituency and their actual concerns about school funding, property assessment fairness, and municipal accountability." },
          { name: "Treating 58% as an Electoral Floor Requiring No Active Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 58% result in a high-education, high-income suburban district with 42% opposition is a working majority, not a demographic inevitability. In Illinois' transformed electoral geography, educated suburban homeowners have demonstrated the capacity to shift based on governance performance and fiscal stewardship concerns. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this district assuming that affluence and homeownership guarantee Republican loyalty, without actively demonstrating fiscal competence and constituent responsiveness, will find the 58% margin vulnerable in the next election cycle." }
