          { name: "Healthcare Access & Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "Healthcare access and cost are concerns that span income levels and education levels; even affluent, well-insured households face rising premiums, prescription drug costs, and anxiety about coverage stability. The Democratic 56% result in a high-income district suggests that healthcare governance — Medicaid expansion, insurance market regulation, prescription drug pricing — has become a salient concern for this constituency. A candidate who can articulate a credible healthcare governance position that addresses affordability and access while respecting market dynamics will engage a primary concern driving the current Democratic advantage." },
          { name: "Tax Philosophy & Progressivity", tag: "careful", why: "Affluent, educated voters at $148,143 income often support progressive tax structures and view public investment as a legitimate use of tax revenue, diverging from the lower-tax-at-all-costs positioning of traditional Republican base voters. The Democratic 56% may reflect preference for tax progressivity over tax reduction, creating a genuine policy disagreement rather than a messaging failure. A candidate addressing this district must engage the actual tax philosophy debate — whether tax burden should be redistributed, whether public investment should be expanded, what the legitimate scope of government spending is — rather than simply advocating lower taxes and hoping educated voters will embrace it." },
          { name: "Assuming This District Will Stay Democratic Because It Is Educated", tag: "avoid", why: "Education level does not determine partisan loyalty; affluent, educated voters are perfectly capable of voting Republican when they perceive Republican governance as serving their material concerns better than Democratic governance. The 56-44 result is not a demographic destiny — it is a current preference that can be shifted by a candidate who identifies and addresses the specific governance concerns driving it. Treating this district as safely Democratic because of college attainment is a strategic mistake that ignores the actual mechanism of voter persuasion." },
          { name: "Deploying Generic Conservative Anti-Government Messaging in a High-Education, High-Income District", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with 63.2% college attainment and $148,143 median income will recognize and reject messaging that treats government as inherently illegitimate or tax reduction as a substitute for actual policy thinking. Generic anti-government rhetoric will read as intellectually unserious to a constituency that evaluates governance claims against actual policy outcomes and evidence. Any candidate seeking to win this district must deploy policy-grounded arguments specific to its concerns, not ideological abstractions that assume low-information voters." }
