          { name: "Treating Affluence as a Substitute for Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, educated constituencies often receive less direct constituent engagement from their own representatives—the assumption being that high income and property values are self-sustaining political assets. In reality, affluent suburban constituents are the most likely to withdraw support if they perceive governance incompetence, missed opportunities for infrastructure or education investment, or inattention to environmental or quality-of-life concerns. The $89,457 median income and 41.2% college education levels mean this constituency has the resources and sophistication to support primary challengers or switch support across district lines if the incumbent underperforms on substantive governance. Affluence is not a substitute for active, serious representation." }
