          { name: "Multiracial Working-Class Coalition & Service Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 53.03% Hispanic, 22.62% white, and 17.54% Black residents, HD-60 is a genuinely multiracial working-class district where coalition-building across communities is essential to governing. The relatively low college attainment (21.6%) and median age of 34.2 suggest a constituency that values representatives who speak to practical economic concerns—healthcare access, job quality, public safety—rather than abstract ideology. The 100% Democratic result indicates baseline partisan alignment, but sustaining that alignment requires demonstrating that government delivers for working people across all three communities simultaneously, not sequentially." },
          { name: "Renter-Majority Constituency & Housing Policy", tag: "careful", why: "At 49.3% renters in a district with only 21.6% college education and $67,808 median income, housing is not an abstract policy issue—it is the primary monthly budget pressure for nearly half the constituents. Renters in this income range are vulnerable to displacement, and any legislative agenda that does not prioritize tenant protections, affordable housing development, and rent regulation risks appearing disconnected from the material reality of the district. The renter majority also tends to turn over, meaning consistent engagement with building permanent relationships with tenant organizations and community groups is necessary to maintain electoral durability." },
          { name: "Taking the 100% Result as Stable", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% Democratic result in a district with 49.3% renters, 21.6% college attainment, and a multiracial working-class composition is not a floor—it is a ceiling that reflects the baseline partisan alignment of Chicago's South Side. The absence of any Republican opposition in the last cycle does not indicate unshakeable loyalty; it indicates an incumbent who has consolidated the Democratic base sufficiently that no serious challenger has emerged. If economic conditions deteriorate, housing costs accelerate, or the representative becomes perceived as unresponsive to renter or working-class concerns, that 100% can erode rapidly in a special election or primary challenge." },
          { name: "Treating Low College Attainment as a Liability Rather Than a Constituent Asset", tag: "avoid", why: "With only 21.6% college-educated adults, HD-60 is a working-class district where credibility comes from demonstrated understanding of working people's actual constraints and priorities, not from educational credentials or abstract policy expertise. A representative who positions educational advancement as the primary pathway to economic mobility—rather than acknowledging that many constituents will not complete college and deserve good jobs, housing security, and dignified work conditions regardless—will appear disconnected from the material reality of most people in the district. The legislative agenda should center working-class economic security, not credentialism." }
