          { name: "Educated Suburban Governance & Service Competence", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 33.2% college attainment, $96,167 income, and 23.1% renter rate define a constituency of educated professionals and stable homeowners for whom government effectiveness—quality schools, property tax fairness, municipal infrastructure, regulated business environment—is the primary evaluation metric, not partisan ideology. These voters chose Mason at 59% based on perceived competence and alignment with their material interests in public goods and functional governance. Demonstrating concrete legislative results on education funding, property tax policy, and economic development that supports professional households maintains this coalition as the stable base of the district." },
          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Tax Policy & Municipal Service Expectations", tag: "careful", why: "The 76.9% homeownership rate and $96,167 median income mean property tax levels, school funding mechanisms, and municipal service delivery directly affect household budgets and asset values for the district's majority. While the Democratic 59% result suggests this constituency currently trusts Democratic governance on these issues, affluent homeowners are mathematically capable of shifting toward Republicans if they perceive tax or regulatory burden as excessive or local services as inadequately funded. This is not a hostile constituency, but it is an economically rational one that evaluates on material impact; maintaining it requires demonstrating that Democratic governance delivers competent, cost-effective service delivery." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as a Safe Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result is a solid margin but not a supermajority or a safe seat—it is a consolidated but not dominant position in a district where 41% of voters actively chose an alternative. In an affluent suburban district with growing Latino population, measurable college education, and homeowner-majority composition, that 41% opposition likely reflects both conservative homeowners skeptical of Democratic tax and regulation positioning and Latino voters not yet fully consolidated around Democratic appeals. Treating the seat as safe rather than as something requiring active consolidation of the Latino community and ongoing service delivery to the affluent base risks erosion." },
          { name: "Overlooking Black Community in Latino-Majority Minority Narrative", tag: "avoid", why: "At 12.23%, the Black population is smaller than the Latino population (28.43%) but large enough to be a meaningful constituency with specific needs around equity, public safety, education, and economic opportunity that can become invisible when district narrative focuses primarily on the largest-growing minority group. A representative who assumes Black support or treats it as background to Latino community engagement rather than as a distinct constituency requiring direct attention and specific policy responsiveness risks both community alienation and the kind of siloed minority representation that erodes Democratic margins in suburban districts." }
