          { name: "Renter Community Visibility & Governance Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 20.4% renter rate represents approximately one-fifth of the district, a population segment with fundamentally different governance priorities than homeowners — housing affordability, tenant protections, transit access, and cost-of-living pressures are direct material concerns for renters in an affluent district where incomes may not keep pace with housing costs. Even in a safe electoral district, renters who feel invisible or unserved in legislative priorities become disengaged constituents. In a district this affluent and educated, renters may include young professionals, service-sector workers, and immigrant families whose needs deserve specific legislative attention rather than absorption into a generic homeowner-majority agenda." },
          { name: "Unopposed Electoral Victory as Risk Signal", tag: "careful", why: "A 100-0 partisan result in any district — even a genuinely safe one — is strategically dangerous if it becomes permission to reduce constituent engagement or assume that the current coalition will hold indefinitely. In IL-HD-62, the absence of electoral competition may mask real constituent dissatisfaction or disengagement among specific groups (renters, Latino residents, younger professionals) who are not being organized by opposition but who are also not being actively served. The 30.38% Latino population and 20.4% renter rate represent constituencies large enough that if a credible challenger emerged, an inattentive incumbent could face genuine electoral risk. The strategic move is to use electoral security to deepen constituent engagement, not to reduce it." },
          { name: "Treating High Income as Universal Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A median household income of $104,781 and 41.6% college attainment signal an affluent, educated constituency, but neither wealth nor education guarantees that residents' actual governance concerns are being met. Affluent, educated residents are often more, not less, demanding consumers of representative government — they expect specificity, follow policy detail, and hold representatives accountable for substantive outcomes. Assuming that high income means contentment, or that the district should be governed for the homeowner majority while renters and Latino residents are secondary constituencies, is a recipe for erosion of genuinely responsive representation." },
          { name: "Conducting Constituent Service Without Relationship-Building Infrastructure", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with significant Latino and renter populations that have no clear institutional voice in legislative governance, constituent service that is purely responsive to individual requests (rather than proactively engaging community organizations and civic leaders) will miss entire populations' needs and reinforce their sense of invisibility. Building lasting constituent relationships in IL-HD-62 requires that the incumbent develop specific institutional partnerships with Latino community organizations, renter advocacy groups, and civic institutions that can surface community priorities that individual constituent requests might never surface. Without that infrastructure, constituent service becomes a service to people who already know how to ask — a self-selecting population that does not reflect the full district." }
