          { name: "Running as a Wealthy Enclave Rather Than a Mixed Community", tag: "avoid", why: "IL-HD-65's high income, high education, and low renter rate can create a rhetorical trap where candidates campaign exclusively to the affluent homeowner base and treat the 12.64% Hispanic, 7.18% Asian, and 2.59% Black constituents as demographic afterthoughts or occasional outreach targets. This framing is both a governance failure and a strategic vulnerability: it leaves the district's minority constituencies without active representation while creating an opening for an opponent who positions herself as serving all constituents, not just the affluent. In a district this homogeneous by class, neglecting the minority communities that do exist is leaving votes and legitimacy on the table." },
          { name: "Treating 56% as a Safe Majority in a Changing Suburban District", tag: "avoid", why: "A 56% result in a district with 49.6% college attainment, high income, and 41.7 median age reflects professional-class economic consensus rather than deep partisan loyalty — these voters evaluate on competence, school quality, and personal economic security. Suburban Illinois districts at this income and education level have shown significant ticket-splitting capacity when they perceive incumbent complacency or when demographic change creates new constituent concerns. Campaigning this seat as though 56% represents a durable floor rather than a current alignment vulnerable to challenger competence and specific constituent engagement will leave an incumbent exposed to a well-resourced opponent who campaigns actively in the district's minority communities and among younger voters concerned about housing affordability." }
