          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "Combined, Black (23.3%) and Hispanic (18.59%) residents represent 41.89% of the district — a near-majority constituency whose voting behavior is directly determinative of whether the 59% holds or contracts. These communities' economic concerns — job access, wage floor, small business lending, neighborhood infrastructure investment — are the material priorities that drive turnout and candidate choice. An incumbent who treats these as 'diverse outreach' constituencies rather than core governing priorities has underestimated the electoral mathematics of the district and created an opening for opposition movement in a constituency that should be reliably Democratic." },
          { name: "White Working-Class Renter and Lower-Income Homeowner Retention", tag: "careful", why: "The 51.49% white share includes both solidly Democratic urban professionals and economically stressed working-class renters and modest-income homeowners responsive to anti-incumbent messaging on public safety, service delivery, and government effectiveness. At 59% in an urban district, the incumbent's margin includes a slice of white working-class voters who are not voting on cultural identity but on whether government is delivering jobs, functioning public services, and safety in their neighborhoods. Loss of even 4-5 percentage points in this subgroup would narrow the margin to 54-55%, and a credible Republican opposition focused on government failure rather than cultural messaging can make that case." },
          { name: "Treating the District's 59% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59% result in a district with 46% renter rate, $48,777 income, and low college attainment (15.5%) is not a secure or stable majority — it is a coalition held by continuous visible attention to constituent economic security. Young renters have no locked-in loyalty; they vote based on immediate cost-of-living effectiveness and can disappear from the electorate in cycles where they lose faith that voting produces change. Any incumbent who campaigns as though the seat is safe or who deprioritizes renter or minority community economic concerns in favor of donor or professional-class priorities is actively creating the vulnerability that converts 59% to 54%." },
          { name: "Ignoring or Minimizing the 41% Republican Opposition Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "At 41% in an urban district of this demographic composition, the Republican opposition is not noise — it is evidence of a credible anti-incumbent message that resonates with a meaningful slice of working-class, public-safety-concerned, or government-skeptical voters. Any incumbent who assumes this opposition is locked in place or not worth serious attention, rather than understanding specifically what drives the 41% and engaging it directly with concrete answers, risks allowing that floor to grow into a majority if conditions shift. The opposition in a 59% Democratic urban district is typically built on government failure narratives; addressing those narratives is how you hold the margin." }
