          { name: "Treating Latino Population as a Peripheral Interest Rather Than Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 21.82% of the district, the Latino population is large enough to be a determinative constituency in a competitive cycle but small enough to become invisible if the incumbent defaults to generic messaging without specific Latino community engagement. Latino voters in working-class districts are responsive to tailored constituent service, representation in hiring, and specific policy focus on immigrant and working-family concerns—not to generic minority outreach. An incumbent who neglects this constituency in favor of abstract Democratic positioning risks losing persuadable voters to opposition messaging that speaks directly to economic concerns." }
