          { name: "Treating 60% as a Stable Majority Rather Than a Vulnerable Position", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 result in a high-income, college-educated district is not a safe seat; it signals meaningful opposition in a demographic cohort that has shifted measurably Democratic over the past decade. Any incumbent or challenger who treats this margin as a comfortable floor rather than a contested outcome will find the actual vote much closer in a competitive cycle. The college-educated professional constituency is responsive to candidate quality and legislative record — not partisan inheritance — and 40% opposition in this demographic is a signal that the seat is genuinely at risk without active constituent engagement." }
