          { name: "Abstract Rhetoric Over Material Delivery on Working-Class Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59% result in a district with 17.2% college attainment, 37.6% renters, and a median household income at $74,634 is not a safe mandate — it is a plurality dependent on whether the incumbent is visibly delivering on the material economic concerns of working-class and working-poor families. Any candidate whose campaign emphasizes partisan messaging, identity appeals, or abstract policy frameworks rather than concrete, named commitments to wage growth, housing affordability, job training, and cost-of-living relief will find that the 41% opposition is insufficient motivation to turn out voters whose primary evaluation is whether government is making their lives materially better." }
