          { name: "Black Community Leadership & Economic Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 27.39%, the Black community is the largest single demographic in HD-80 — nearly equal to the white population at 51.83% — and represents the core of the Democratic 59% margin. Black families in this district face specific economic challenges: homeownership barriers, wealth gaps, employment discrimination, and school funding inequities are the institutional concerns that shape their household security. A representative who demonstrates concrete engagement with Black-led community organizations, churches, and local businesses — and a legislative record on workforce development, lending equity, and school investment — builds the authentic relationship that sustains a 59% margin." },
          { name: "Immigration Status & Latino Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 16.87%, the Hispanic/Latino community is a growing and economically vulnerable constituency in HD-80, with workforce participation heavily concentrated in service, construction, and agricultural sectors where wage theft, unsafe conditions, and documentation status create legal and financial exposure. The 59% Democratic margin may reflect some Latino support, but without specific engagement around workplace rights, immigration status protections, and language-accessible services, this constituency can be taken for granted or mobilized by competing campaigns. Authentic partnership with Latino-led organizations and direct communication on economic and legal protections is essential to holding this community's support." },
          { name: "Treating the 41% Opposition as a Stable Opposition Rather Than a Swing Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59% Democratic result in a Chicago-area district is strong but not overwhelming — the 41% Republican opposition in a working-class, heavily renter, majority-minority district cannot be dismissed as ideologically locked. Some portion of that 41% likely consists of white homeowners concerned about property taxes, older residents with conservative social views, or working-class voters persuadable on economic grounds. Any candidate who campaigns as though 59% is a safe majority and ignores the opposition's material concerns is vulnerable to a challenger who addresses housing costs, school quality, or public safety more credibly." },
          { name: "Portraying Minority Communities' Economic Concerns as 'Special Interests' Rather Than Constituent Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 44.26% of the population is Black or Latino, framing workplace protections, lending equity, school funding, or housing policy as 'special interest' positions rather than core constituent governance is both analytically wrong and politically self-defeating. These communities comprise nearly half the district's population and drive the Democratic coalition's electoral foundation. Any rhetoric or legislative record that suggests these communities' economic security is secondary to other priorities will erode the authentic constituent relationship that sustains a 59% margin and opens the district to credible opposition." }
