          { name: "Economic Mobility and Housing Affordability for Younger Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 38.8 median age and 28.5% renter rate, HD-101 contains a substantial population of younger adults and renters whose primary economic concerns are housing costs, wage growth, and pathway to homeownership—issues that directly affect quality of life but are often secondary in districts with older, homeowner-majority constituencies. The $70,947 median household income, while solid, must cover rising housing, education, and healthcare costs in an inflationary environment where young adults' wage growth frequently lags. An incumbent who leads on housing affordability policy, workforce development, and the economic conditions that allow younger adults to stay in the district is addressing the constituency segment most likely to generate electoral challenge in a future cycle." },
          { name: "Educational Access and Workforce Development Pipeline", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 27.5% college attainment—below state and national averages—HD-101's educational infrastructure and workforce training systems are directly consequential for constituent economic mobility. Community college affordability, vocational training partnerships, and the economic pipeline that allows young adults to build careers without leaving the district are material governance issues, not abstract talking points. An incumbent who engages educational institutions and workforce development programs with concrete policy support is addressing a long-term constituency concern that opposition could easily weaponize if education access remains peripheral to the legislative agenda." },
          { name: "Hispanic and Black Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "At 5.1% Hispanic and 2.1% Black, HD-101 has meaningful minority populations whose specific economic and social concerns can become invisible in a district where the dominant narrative focuses on the white-majority constituency. Small business support, healthcare access, education opportunity for minority youth, and economic mobility are the concrete concerns of these communities, and they deserve specific constituent attention rather than assumption into the broader working-class narrative. An unopposed incumbent has no electoral pressure to demonstrate engagement with minority communities, but that absence of pressure is precisely the vulnerability that a credible challenger could exploit." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Result as Evidence of Durable Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in a Republican district tells you about electoral dominance in a specific cycle—it does not tell you whether younger adults, renters, Hispanic residents, or economically stressed working-class households feel adequately represented on issues that matter to them. The absence of opposition is not the same as constituent satisfaction; it is the absence of a mechanism to express discontent. Any incumbent who interprets this result as permission to deprioritize constituent engagement with younger adults, renters, and minority communities is building the conditions for future electoral vulnerability." },
          { name: "Assuming the White-Majority Narrative Encompasses All Constituency Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "While 89% of the district is white, the 5.1% Hispanic, 2.1% Black, and ~3% other populations represent real people with distinct economic and social concerns that cannot be adequately addressed through a monolithic working-class or homeowner narrative. Treating minority communities as secondary constituencies—or assuming their concerns are automatically aligned with the majority population's priorities—is both a governance failure and an electoral risk in a district where minority voters have no current mechanism to signal preference for alternative representation." }
