          { name: "Household Economic Security & Middle-Class Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $64,795 median household income with 33.8% renters and 40.1 median age, HD-104 is a stable middle-class constituency evaluating whether government is protecting their economic position. Property tax burden, school funding, housing affordability, healthcare costs, and wage-growth policy are the concrete household concerns that drive voting in a tied district. A candidate who articulates a specific vision for middle-class economic security and demonstrates it through constituent service and legislative record moves from 50% to 51%." },
          { name: "Constituent Service & Government Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "A perfectly tied district at 50-50 does not split on ideology — it splits on the perception of whether the current representative is solving problems for actual constituents. Specific case resolutions, demonstrated responsiveness to constituent requests, and visible investment in community needs are the currency that moves swing voters in a district where partisan identity is weak. The candidate who establishes a reputation for actually helping constituents with tangible problems has the strategic advantage in a tied race." },
          { name: "Renter & Homeowner Constituency Differentiation", tag: "careful", why: "The 33.8% renter rate is substantial and represents a distinct constituency from the 66.2% homeowner majority — but both groups are present and both evaluate household economic security. Renters prioritize housing affordability and neighborhood stability; homeowners prioritize property tax fairness and school funding. Any candidate who focuses only on homeowner concerns implicitly cedes the renter swing vote to a challenger who articulates affordable housing policy with specificity. In a 50-50 district, that swing vote is the margin." },
          { name: "Partisan Base Consolidation Without Opposition Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "The current representative's 50% reflects consolidation of roughly half the district's voters — a coalition that is not large enough to win in a competitive general election. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns exclusively to the existing base while ignoring the 50% opposition is making a fundamental arithmetic error; that opposition vote is persuadable and its defection is the margin between 50% and losing. In a tied district, base consolidation is a losing strategy." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative or Progressive Positioning Without Material Governance Answers", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-104's 50-50 split in a district without strong ideological tilt suggests that candidates who lead with cultural or abstract partisan messaging are not reaching the persuadable majority. Voters in a tied middle-class district are evaluating whether candidates will deliver on tangible household concerns — school quality, property tax fairness, housing affordability, job creation — not whether they align with national partisan movements. A candidate who campaigns on ideology rather than material governance for middle-class households is abandoning the strategic terrain where this election is actually decided." }
