          { name: "Educational Access and Youth Retention", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.6% college-attainment rate combined with a 42.4-year median age suggests limited local educational pathways and possible out-migration of younger adults seeking degree or career opportunities. In a district where one in five households may have school-age children, the quality and accessibility of K-12 education, vocational training, and affordable higher education (or pathways to nearby institutions) are direct economic concerns for families evaluating whether to stay. A legislator who champions workforce development, career technical education, and college affordability is addressing the material reason young people leave and building durable support among families with children." },
          { name: "Demographic Transition and Renter Community Invisibility", tag: "careful", why: "The 21.9% renter rate in what is likely an exurban or small-town community suggests demographic composition that may not be politically organized or accustomed to having its interests articulated by elected officials. Renters, younger adults, and the ~2.6% AIAN gap are constituencies that can be rendered invisible by a representative focused exclusively on the homeowner majority. In a competitive election, an opponent who speaks directly to renters' concerns (affordable housing, tenant protections) or to younger adults' concerns (job quality, affordability) could rapidly mobilize these constituencies if the incumbent has not engaged them affirmatively." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Victory Indicates Long-Term Political Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in one election cycle is a tactical victory, not evidence of durable coalition support — particularly in a district with 21.9% renters, 42.4-year median age, and limited college attainment, demographics that often indicate demographic flux and lower incumbent-loyalty signals. An incumbent who interprets 100% as proof of permanent security and relaxes constituent engagement is vulnerable to a credible challenger who speaks to the material concerns of renters, younger voters, or working-class households evaluating affordability and opportunity. The absence of opposition is an opportunity to build affirmative relationships, not permission to assume the base is locked." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Abstract Conservative Positioning Without Economic Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "A district at $73,720 median income with 21.9% renters and 22.6% college attainment is not primarily motivated by abstract ideological positioning — it is motivated by material economic concerns: whether costs are rising faster than wages, whether there are jobs and educational opportunities for younger residents, whether the community is a stable place to build a family. A legislator who campaigns on general conservative themes without speaking specifically to affordability, job quality, educational access, and the conditions that make communities economically viable is ceding ground to any opponent who offers concrete answers to concrete economic questions." }
