          "The Hispanic majority and the renter plurality represent two constituencies with overlapping but distinct governance expectations. The Hispanic community — 54% of the district — has economic concerns rooted in wage stability, workplace protections, immigration policy affecting family members and neighbors, and educational opportunity for children, alongside cultural and language-access expectations in municipal services. The renter plurality — 52.6% of the district, with significant overlap with the Hispanic community — has direct financial exposure to housing costs, eviction protections, and the local regulatory environment that shapes whether housing is affordable or extractive. A representative who addresses Hispanic economic concerns while ignoring renters' housing-cost burden is missing the material reality of households where renters and Spanish-speakers are often the same people. The $86,120 median income, while respectable on a statewide basis, translates to monthly housing costs that consume 25-35% of household income for renters in an urban market; that constraint is the lived economic reality that drives political evaluation in IL-SD-2 far more than state-level partisan messaging."
