          { name: "Renter Economics and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 67.1% of the district renting — more than two-thirds — housing cost burden is the single most pressing economic issue for the constituency's dominant majority. At $70,682 median household income in what is likely an urban or inner-suburban market, rent as a percentage of income is a direct material constraint on quality of life, savings capacity, and economic mobility. Tenant protections, rent stabilization, affordable housing development, and the relationship between local wage growth and rental market inflation are the concrete policy levers that directly affect the economic security of two-thirds of households. Any representative who does not build a specific, demonstrable record on renter economics is governing without addressing the primary economic concern of the district's majority." },
          { name: "Educated Workforce Economic Opportunity and Student Debt", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 59.7% college-educated rate is the second-highest educational attainment level in Illinois and signals a constituency that has invested in education expecting economic return. Student loan debt, professional employment opportunity, wage growth relative to cost of living, and the specific barriers that prevent educated young adults from building wealth and stability are the governance issues this constituency evaluates on. At 36.4-year median age and likely concentrated in professional services, technology, healthcare, and education sectors, the educated workforce's concerns about career mobility, benefits stability, and the sustainability of professional employment are not abstract — they are the lived experience of a majority of the district. A legislative agenda that treats education-to-employment mobility and debt sustainability as governance priorities rather than left-wing preoccupations better reflects the actual constituency." },
          { name: "Multi-Racial Constituency Coalition-Building", tag: "careful", why: "With no demographic majority — 52.11% white, 17.3% Hispanic/Latino, 16.67% Black, 9.11% Asian — the district requires authentic coalition-building across racial and ethnic communities rather than silent deference to the white plurality. Each of these communities has specific economic concerns, healthcare access needs, and community-specific governance issues that deserve direct attention rather than being absorbed into generic minority-community messaging. A representative who cannot demonstrate specific knowledge of and engagement with the distinct needs of the Black community, the Latino community, and the Asian community across multiple issue areas will find credibility eroding among constituencies that evaluate on competence and specific delivery rather than inherited partisan alignment." },
          { name: "Treating 100% Unopposed Status as Permission to Neglect Specific Policy Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed Democratic victory in IL-SD-7 reflects Republican organizational absence rather than Democratic invulnerability or sustainable electoral dominance. Any incumbent or challenger who treats a 100% result as permission to govern without specific policy attention to renter economics, educated workforce mobility, and the multi-racial coalition's distinct needs will find that assumption tested in the next competitive cycle. The unopposed status is a current advantage, not a permanent condition — it reflects the absence of organized opposition at this particular moment, not the absence of underlying competitiveness in a district defined by evaluation on policy competence and material delivery." },
          { name: "Framing Housing and Renter Issues as 'Special Interests' Rather Than Majority Constituency Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 67.1% rent and housing cost burden is the dominant economic issue for most households, framing tenant protections or affordable housing development as accommodations to special interests rather than basic governance priorities for the majority constituency misreads the district's actual structure. Any candidate or representative who positions homeowner property-tax concerns or free-market housing approaches as the default and renter economics as a competing interest group will find that framing actively hostile to the material reality of two-thirds of households. At this demographic composition and income level, renter economics are not a special interest — they are the governance baseline." }
