          { name: "Treating 76% as a Durable Majority Requiring No Active Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76% result in a district with 29.5% renters, $119,539 median income, and 68.5% college education reflects voter confidence in current governance, not an inherited Democratic supermajority that will sustain itself through demography alone. Affluent, educated, mobile voters are responsive to governance performance and willing to express dissatisfaction through turnout reduction or candidate switching if they perceive that government is not delivering on material quality-of-life concerns. Any candidate or incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 76% represents a safe baseline rather than a result that must be actively earned through continued governance competence will discover that the margin erodes more quickly than partisan data suggests." },
          { name: "Abstract Partisan Messaging as a Substitute for Specific Governance Proposals", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 68.5% of voters are college-educated, partisan boilerplate and abstract ideological positioning resonate weakly; voters here have the educational capital to evaluate specific policy proposals and their likely effects, and they expect candidates to engage at that level of detail. A campaign that relies on broad Democratic messaging about values and principles without articulating specific, evidence-based approaches to housing, education, transit, and fiscal sustainability will fail to maintain voter engagement with a constituency that evaluates government through the lens of expertise and results. This is a district that requires candidates to do the work of detailed policy articulation, not one where party label alone carries sufficient weight." }
