          { name: "Skilled Trades & Non-College Pathways in an Educated District", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 38.5% college attainment, 61.5% of adults in IL-SD-10 lack a bachelor's degree—a substantial constituency whose economic security depends on vocational training, apprenticeships, and skilled-trades viability rather than white-collar pathways. At $91,955 median income, this cohort has achieved middle-class stability but often through non-college routes, making credential recognition, apprenticeship program funding, and trades industry partnership direct legislative concerns. A candidate who engages this constituency with specificity on workforce development demonstrates attention to a majority of the district that college-focused education rhetoric may overlook." },
          { name: "Asian Community Representation & Credentialing Access", tag: "careful", why: "Asian residents represent 7.14% of IL-SD-10—a meaningful minority often absent from majority-focused political engagement despite their presence in affluent, educated districts. Asian immigrant families and professionals frequently have specific concerns around credential recognition, professional licensing alignment, and educational access for children that differ from broader majority-community framings. Authentic engagement on these issues, rather than treating Asian residents as a component of a generic 'minority coalition,' builds genuine constituent relationship and prevents this group from becoming invisible in legislative priority-setting." },
          { name: "Treating 61% as a Durable Ceiling Rather Than a Floor Requiring Active Defense", tag: "avoid", why: "A 61-39 result in a district with $91,955 median income and 38.5% college attainment is not a landslide or even a secure majority—it reflects a constituency that evaluates actively on economic performance. The 39% opposition in this affluent, educated district is sufficiently large and strategically coherent to pose a real threat if the incumbent neglects the specific economic concerns of Hispanic families, non-college-educated workers, and Asian professionals. Campaigning as though 61% is locked in without continued active engagement of these communities misreads the district's actual strategic situation." },
          { name: "Defaulting to National Cultural Issues While Neglecting Local Economic Constituency Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where Hispanic residents comprise over one-quarter of the population and skilled-trades workers comprise a substantial portion of the economy, a legislative or campaign agenda focused primarily on national cultural debates rather than education costs, housing affordability, and workforce development risks losing the specific constituencies whose engagement produces the 61% result. Hispanic families and working-class homeowners in IL-SD-10 evaluate on whether government is delivering for their family's economic security; national-issue framing that neglects these material concerns leaves the incumbent vulnerable to a challenger offering concrete local answers." }
