          { name: "Renter Economic Security & Housing Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.2% renter occupancy paired with $57,038 median income, a substantial portion of IL-SD-16's constituency is experiencing housing cost pressure sufficient to constrain other household spending and create vulnerability to displacement. Tenant protections, anti-predatory lending enforcement, affordable housing development, and anti-displacement initiatives are not peripheral quality-of-life issues — they are foundational economic security concerns for the district's working families. An incumbent with an unopposed seat has rare opportunity to advance these policies without partisan defense costs; deploying that safety on behalf of visible housing-stability improvements builds constituent loyalty rooted in material improvement rather than partisan default." },
          { name: "Wage Standards & Worker Protections in Service-Sector & Care Economy", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 19% college attainment, IL-SD-16's workforce is concentrated in jobs — retail, food service, care work, transportation — where four-year degrees are not the pathway and where wage floor standards, working-condition enforcement, and protection against wage theft are the primary determinants of family economic security. State-level wage standards, wage-theft enforcement, care-worker protections, and accessible grievance mechanisms for low-wage workers are legislative priorities that directly address the material conditions of most district residents. An incumbent who advances these issues with specificity and documentation is building a legislative record that demonstrates concrete delivery for the constituency." },
          { name: "Majority-Black & Majority-Latino Community Specific Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 49.38% Black and 24.93% Latino (together 74% of the district), these communities are not constituencies to be aggregated into a generic 'minority' voting bloc — they have distinct economic concerns, distinct historical relationships to state institutions, and distinct evaluation criteria for representation. The Black community's experience with predatory housing practices, wage discrimination, and law enforcement requires specific policy attention; the Latino community's concerns around workplace rights, immigrant-family economic stability, and language access require distinct engagement. An unopposed incumbent faces elevated risk of treating these communities as a consolidated base rather than distinct constituencies with specific grievances, and that abstraction will eventually generate challenger openings." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Permission to Neglect Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election is not a license to reduce district presence or assume constituent loyalty; it is an artifact of the district's Democratic structure, not evidence of constituent satisfaction with material conditions. In a district facing housing instability, wage pressures, and limited economic mobility, an incumbent who uses the unopposed status to focus on statewide issues or higher-profile agendas while constituent conditions stagnate will find that the absence of current partisan opposition does not prevent future challenger emergence rooted in constituent frustration." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Higher-Education or Higher-Income Constituency Priorities", tag: "avoid", why: "The 19% college attainment rate and $57k median income are not districts' weaknesses to be overcome through education policy — they are the primary demographic reality of the constituency. An incumbent who campaigns on higher-education access, student-debt relief, or professional-class economic concerns while the district's renters, wage workers, and service-sector families face immediate housing and wage pressures is misallocating political capital and generating the impression that representation serves constituencies outside the district rather than those within it." }
