          { name: "School Quality & Educational Opportunity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 35.5% college-educated rate and 36.8-year median age signal a district of families actively evaluating schools and the pathway from K-12 to economic mobility. Hispanic and white families alike have children in the district's schools and judge their elected representatives partly on school funding, teacher quality, and college-access outcomes. This is an issue that transcends demographic divisions and where specific legislative achievement — budget priorities, accountability mechanisms, college-prep programs — directly affects parent behavior and loyalty." },
          { name: "White Voter Defection & Coalition Maintenance", tag: "careful", why: "At 41.76% white population, white voters represent two-fifths of the electorate and are currently splitting 59-41 Democratic — meaning 41% of white voters are actively choosing the Republican alternative. Whether this defection is driven by school quality frustration, tax burden concern, ideological positioning, or specific local governance failures is the critical strategic question that determines whether the incumbent's coalition is sustainable or perpetually at risk. Understanding the drivers of white opposition with specificity is the prerequisite for either consolidating these voters or acknowledging that they are a hard loss and focusing on Hispanic turnout and preference." },
          { name: "Treating 59% as a Safe Democratic Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 59-41 result in a district where the two largest demographic groups are nearly equal in size (40.56% Hispanic, 41.76% white) is not a safe Democratic seat — it is a coalition-dependent seat where Hispanic turnout and preference are delivering the margin. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 59% represents a stable floor rather than an active coalition to be maintained will find that coalition at risk if Hispanic constituents conclude that economic promises are not being delivered or if a credible Republican challenger emerges with genuine solutions on housing, schools, or job quality." },
          { name: "Framing as a Partisan Referendum Rather Than Household Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "IL-SD-25 voters at $95,585 median income are evaluating their state senator primarily on whether government is delivering tangible improvements to their household economic security — housing costs, school quality, job opportunity — not on abstract partisan messaging. Any candidate who defaults to partisan positioning without specific solutions to the material concerns that drive household budget decisions is ignoring the actual decision-making framework of a middle-class, working-age constituency. This district responds to solutions, not slogans." }
