          "Consolidate college-educated suburban voters as the election-decisive coalition: the 50.3% college-attainment rate, 50.3% female plurality (implicit in suburban demographic profile), and recent ticket-splitting patterns among educated women make this group the core Democratic strength; messaging on education, democracy, and climate should activate this constituency at high turnout.",
          "Do not take the 58-42 result as a floor: a 16-point margin is substantial, but in a district where the median income is $102,787 and educational attainment is high, a credible challenger with investment in Hispanic persuasion and fiscal messaging could create genuine competitive pressure in a way that current partisan alignment may obscure.",
          "Serve the renter minority (30.5%) with specific housing and affordability attention: renters in affluent suburbs often have different economic vulnerabilities than homeowners, and neglecting this 30.5% to focus exclusively on homeowner property interests creates an opening for a challenger to build coalition among younger, less-wealthy district residents."
