          { name: "Asian and Hispanic Communities in Diverse Urban Setting", tag: "lean-into", why: "Combined, Asian (17.42%) and Hispanic (17.36%) residents comprise over one-third of IL-SD-28's population — a meaningful plurality that, alongside the 57.66% white majority, makes this a genuinely diverse district. These communities are not peripheral to the district's economic or civic life; they are integrated into a high-income, education-heavy community with distinct perspectives on education access, housing affordability, and economic mobility. Authentic engagement with Asian and Hispanic constituents — not as separate outreach but as full members of the district's economic and civic conversation — is both a governance obligation and a political necessity in a district this diverse." },
          { name: "Unopposed Victory and Electoral Complacency", tag: "careful", why: "Murphy's unopposed election (100% Democratic, 0% Republican) reflects the district's strong Democratic alignment but masks the underlying electoral dynamics that could become vulnerable if constituent engagement lapses. An unopposed victory in a high-income, educated, diverse urban district can create a false sense of security; these constituencies are politically sophisticated and will mobilize alternative representation if they conclude an incumbent is not delivering on their specific priorities around housing, schools, environmental quality, and fiscal management. Treating 100% as a floor rather than a symptom of current alignment is the strategic error most likely to produce a credible challenger in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Taking Affluent Urban Loyalty for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "High-income, college-educated urban constituencies are politically volatile precisely because they have the resources and information to evaluate performance carefully and the flexibility to switch representation if dissatisfied. A legislator who assumes Democratic loyalty in IL-SD-28 because of the district's partisan lean, rather than continuously demonstrating specific delivery on the district's material priorities, creates the opening for a challenger who offers more effective constituent service on housing costs, school quality, or property tax management." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 30.6% Renter Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 30.6% renter rate, nearly one-third of IL-SD-28 households do not own property and face distinct economic constraints around housing cost, eviction risk, and access to stable neighborhoods. An incumbent who frames all district priorities through a homeowner lens — property taxes, school funding tied to residential property values, neighborhood stability as property protection — effectively represents less than 70% of the actual constituency. Renters' concerns around housing affordability, tenant protections, and economic mobility are material district issues that require explicit legislative attention, not absorption into homeowner-focused framing." }
