          { name: "Anti-Suburban Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "Any candidate who frames suburban quality-of-life concerns — property values, school funding, municipal service delivery — as selfish NIMBYism or exclusionary will find that framing actively hostile to the material reality of 80% homeowners who have built economic security through property ownership. In a district this affluent and this educated, positioning against suburban interests is self-defeating; the 44% opposition to the current Republican is not based on anti-suburban sentiment but on specific concerns about whether the current representative is addressing their actual governance priorities effectively." },
          { name: "Partisan Activation Over Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "IL-SD-32's educated, affluent, 41.2-year-old constituency does not respond predictably to partisan mobilization; they evaluate on whether their specific concerns — school quality, property tax fairness, local infrastructure — are being addressed with competence. A campaign that relies on partisan talking points rather than concrete answers to local governance questions will underperform the 56% result in a district where 44% of voters have already expressed skepticism. This electorate respects evidence and specific policy knowledge; partisan activation without constituent service delivery will not consolidate the base or persuade the marginal voter." }
