          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Tax and Fiscal Policy", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $107,831 median household income, 87% homeownership, and college-educated majority, IL-SD-35 is a constituency whose material interests center on property-tax management, fiscal efficiency, and regulatory burden on household wealth accumulation. Republican positioning on taxes and regulation has successfully consolidated this constituency; any challenger must offer credible alternative policy on property-tax fairness and service efficiency, not abstract ideology. The unopposed result reflects successful Republican messaging on these issues, but unopposed incumbency creates space for Democratic messaging to be ignored—meaning a credible challenger could reframe tax policy around fairness and efficiency rather than partisan doctrine." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Mobility and Integration", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 13.51% of the district population, the Hispanic/Latino community represents a meaningful constituency whose engagement—or neglect—materially affects electoral outcomes in an affluent suburban context. This population likely includes both service-sector workers and a professional/entrepreneurial cohort; both groups have specific interests in housing policy, business regulation, and economic opportunity. The unopposed Republican result may mask limited Democratic engagement with this constituency and insufficient Republican attention to whether Hispanic households perceive their economic interests as central to governing priorities. A candidate who speaks credibly to Hispanic business development, housing policy, and educational opportunity while maintaining affluent-homeowner messaging can consolidate both constituencies." },
          { name: "Education Funding and Service Quality for Affluent Communities", tag: "careful", why: "In a district with 39.7% college attainment and $107,831 median income, education quality and property-tax-funded public services are constituent expectations, not aspirations. Affluent suburban voters evaluate education policy on measurable outcomes and service efficiency, not partisan rhetoric; Democrats can credibly challenge Republicans on whether current funding levels and accountability mechanisms are delivering adequate education quality. The unopposed result reflects successful Republican consolidation of this issue, but Democrats who frame education funding as both a fiscal and equity issue could appeal to college-educated voters' values around opportunity and institutional quality." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Incumbency as Evidence of Durable Dominance", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% Republican result without Democratic opposition is a consequence of Democratic resource allocation away from this seat, not evidence that voters prefer Republican governance on the merits. In an affluent, college-educated district, the absence of organized opposition creates the false impression of consensus; in reality, the incumbent holds the seat by default. Any candidate who campaigns on the assumption that this constituency's Republican loyalty is inherited rather than contingent on material delivery will find that assumption tested by a credible challenger." },
          { name: "Neglecting Demographic Diversification and Younger Adult Constituencies", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed result reflects successful Republican consolidation of affluent, older, Anglo homeowners; it may mask significant disaffection among younger adult households and increasingly diverse communities in the district. At 13.51% Hispanic and 3.47% Asian, demographic diversification is measurable and trending; if the incumbent's governing record shows inattention to these communities' economic and civic concerns, a Democratic challenger who builds authentic relationships with these constituencies while maintaining credible messaging on affluent-homeowner interests can shift the electoral baseline. Assuming that unopposed victory translates to voter satisfaction across all constituencies is strategically dangerous." }
