          { name: "Wage Growth and Economic Opportunity for Working-Class Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.7-year median age and 23.8% college attainment indicate a working-class constituency at the prime wage-earning years; economic evaluations will rest on whether household incomes are growing in real terms or stagnating. State-level policy on minimum wage, workforce development, childcare cost assistance, and small business access directly affects working-class economic mobility; a Democratic incumbent who can demonstrate that state policy has improved wage-level opportunity is defending the seat on materially defensible ground." },
          { name: "Hispanic and Black Community Service and Economic Inclusion", tag: "careful", why: "At 11.9% Hispanic and 9.92% Black, these communities represent roughly 22% of the district and are large enough to shift a 55-45 margin if turnout drops or candidate preference shifts; their specific concerns around wage equity, business ownership access, and equitable public services can be subsumed into generalized working-class messaging and become invisible. Active constituent engagement with the Hispanic and Black communities—rather than assuming they will turn out automatically—is required to maintain Democratic performance in this district." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Safe Democratic Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 10-point margin in a working-class district where 32.6% of residents are renters and incomes are $61,704 is not commanding; it reflects a coalition that will evaluate Democratic incumbents on material economic delivery, not on inherited partisan alignment. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 55% represents a comfortable hold—rather than engaging the 45% opposition's economic concerns and consolidating renter and working-class support through specific policy—is vulnerable to a well-executed Republican challenge." },
          { name: "Cultural or Identity-Based Positioning Over Economic Materialism", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 23.8% college attainment and $61,704 median income, abstract cultural or identity-based positioning does not resonate as effectively as concrete economic policy; a Democratic incumbent who defaults to cultural messaging at the expense of wage growth, housing affordability, and childcare cost policy is ceding the district's actual concerns to potential challengers. Working-class voters evaluate on material outcomes; Democratic messaging that emphasizes cultural identity rather than economic delivery is strategically counterproductive in IL-SD-36." }
