          { name: "Suburban Professional Constituency Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "With $79,700 median income, 28.3% college attainment, and a 36.7 median age, SD-38 is an educated, economically secure suburban district where voters evaluate on schools, healthcare access, tax policy, and whether government delivers tangible quality-of-life improvements. School funding adequacy, property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, and infrastructure maintenance are the material concerns that determine whether this constituency stays engaged with the Republican incumbent or becomes available to a credible alternative. A representative who speaks fluently to these specific suburban quality-of-life issues, rather than abstract partisan messaging, commands the credibility this district's constituency demands." },
          { name: "Young Renters and Economic Mobility in Suburban Illinois", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 29.6% renter rate represents nearly one-third of the district's households — a cohort with materially different economic concerns than the homeowner majority. Young renters in suburban Illinois face housing cost burdens, limited wealth-building pathways, and economic instability that make them responsive to candidates who offer concrete solutions around housing affordability, tenant protections, and economic opportunity. In a district where the incumbent currently faces no opposition, visibility on renter issues and constituent service to this population is the most direct way to inoculate against future Democratic recruitment of a cohort that represents a large and growing share of suburban households." },
          { name: "College-Educated and Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 28.3% college-educated population and 20.6% combined Hispanic and Black population are constituencies whose civic engagement and political loyalty are contingent rather than inherited — they evaluate on whether the representative actively serves their specific needs around education, economic opportunity, and fair treatment. The current unopposed status does not indicate satisfaction among these communities; it indicates they have not yet organized an alternative. Consistent visibility and constituent service to these populations is essential to retain them and prevent future Democratic investment in challenging the seat among demographics most responsive to alternative messaging." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Structural Security", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election result in IL-SD-38 reflects current political conditions and the absence of Democratic investment, not a permanent structural Republican majority in the district. Suburban districts nationally have been trending toward greater political competitiveness as education levels rise and demographic change accelerates. A representative who interprets unopposed status as evidence of secure tenure and reduces constituent engagement, constituent service, or visibility to younger, college-educated, and minority populations is actively creating conditions for future electoral vulnerability." },
          { name: "Ignoring Renter and Young Adult Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "With nearly 30% renters and a median age of 36.7, SD-38 contains substantial populations of younger adults and renters whose economic concerns around housing, healthcare costs, and economic mobility are real and present. A representative whose legislative record or public messaging shows no engagement with these constituencies' material concerns effectively concedes their future availability to a Democratic challenger who does. In suburban districts, renters and younger professionals are swing constituencies whose turnout and candidate preference decisions are not predetermined by geography or party affiliation." }
