        dem: 49, rep: 51,
        partisanSub: "Harriss won with 51%.",
        demos: [
          { label: "White", pct: 76.38, color: "#4e9e68" },
          { label: "Black / African American", pct: 12.15, color: "#2563eb" },
          { label: "Hispanic / Latino", pct: 5.54, color: "#f59e0b" },
          { label: "Asian", pct: 1.29, color: "#ef4444" }
        ],
        issues: [
          { name: "Middle-Class Household Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $69,892 median income with 69.2% homeownership (implied by 30.8% renters) and 39.2 median age, IL-SD-56 is a working and professional middle-class suburban constituency where property tax burden, school funding adequacy, and housing affordability are the direct household concerns that activate voters. In a district where the partisan margin is only two points, a legislative agenda that reduces or stabilizes property tax burden and delivers measurable school improvements commands credibility with the constituency most likely to swing between parties. The combination of homeowner property-tax concerns and renter affordability pressures creates a unified message: government should reduce the cost of living in this district, not increase it." },
          { name: "School Quality and Local Education Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 39.2 median age and 69.2% homeownership, IL-SD-56 includes substantial numbers of families with school-age children and older homeowners whose property values are tied to school district reputation. School funding, teacher quality, class sizes, and whether local schools are improving or declining are the concrete measures by which suburban households evaluate whether their tax dollars are being spent effectively. In a 51-49 district, a candidate with a specific, credible record on education investment and visible school improvement has a direct pathway to consolidating the parent and younger family voter base that determines swing outcomes." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 5.5% Hispanic population in a 51-49 suburban district represents a measurable swing constituency whose household concerns — housing costs, school quality, economic mobility for their children — are identical to the broader suburban middle-class agenda. However, Hispanic suburban voters in competitive districts also respond to whether they perceive authentic engagement from candidates or are being treated as an afterthought added to a primarily white-focused campaign. At 5.5%, the Hispanic community is significant enough to swing a two-point margin, and their response to genuine economic messaging versus token acknowledgment will determine whether they vote with the incumbent coalition or against it." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture War Positioning", tag: "avoid", why: "IL-SD-56 is a suburban district where households are focused on schools, property taxes, and local economic stability — not abstract culture-war positioning that plays well in primary elections but alienates swing voters with children in public schools and renters concerned about affordability. A candidate who campaigns on cultural divisiveness rather than concrete household economic concerns will lose the 30.8% renter constituency and younger families who have material reasons to be skeptical of partisan ideology, particularly in a district this closely divided where a small swing determines the outcome." },
          { name: "Treating 51% as a Durable Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 51-49 result in a suburban district is not a coalition to be managed passively; it is a narrow plurality that will flip in the next cycle if the incumbent becomes complacent about delivering results on property tax management, school quality, and cost-of-living concerns. An incumbent who campaigns this seat as if 51% is a comfortable hold rather than a fragile majority won by aligning with swing constituencies on material household concerns will find their margin eroded quickly when an opponent offers a credible alternative on those same issues." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "IL-SD-56: A Tossup Suburban District at 51-49, Where Middle-Income Homeowners and a 5.5% Hispanic Swing Constituency Determine Control",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "IL-SD-56 is a genuinely competitive district where a 51-49 Republican margin conceals an electorate actively divided between two nearly equal coalitions. The demographic profile — 76.4% white, 69.2% homeownership implied by 30.8% renters, $69,892 median household income, and 39.2 median age — describes a solidly middle-class suburban constituency of working and professional households with real economic stakes in property values, schools, and local service delivery. This is not a district where partisan loyalty is inherited; it is a district where households evaluate candidates on whether they deliver concrete results on the issues that affect their daily lives. The 51% Republican result in 2024 should be read not as a stable coalition but as a narrow plurality in a district where either party has genuine capacity to build a majority if they understand what their constituency actually cares about.",
          "The most significant strategic fact about IL-SD-56 is the 5.5% Hispanic population in a district where the reported four demographic categories account for 95.4% of residents. This 4.6% gap is likely a combination of underreporting in Census categories and genuine demographic diversity, but the Hispanic community at 5.5% — concentrated in suburban residential areas where housing costs and school quality drive household decisions — represents a measurable swing constituency whose electoral behavior is responsive to economic messaging rather than fixed by partisan identity. The 29.8% college attainment rate is slightly below the state average, and the $69,892 median income places the district squarely in middle-class suburban territory where property tax burden, school funding, and local cost-of-living pressures are the material concerns that activate voters. Any candidate who wants to hold or win IL-SD-56 must build a governing agenda around middle-class economic security and school quality — not abstract ideology — and engage the Hispanic community and younger renters with specific answers about housing affordability and economic opportunity, not assumed partisan alignment."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat 51-49 as a genuine tossup, not a stable Republican hold: the two-point margin in a suburban district where both parties have organizational capacity means the next cycle will turn on whether the incumbent has delivered visible results on middle-class household concerns — property taxes, schools, local infrastructure — not on partisan brand.",
          "Lead with middle-class economic security: property tax fairness, school funding adequacy, and housing affordability are the primary household concerns for a $69,892-income, 30.8% renter constituency where younger families and fixed-income homeowners both face real cost pressures.",
          "Engage the Hispanic community and younger renters with specific economic messaging: the 5.5% Hispanic population and 30.8% renter rate are swing constituencies responsive to concrete answers about housing costs, education quality, and whether government is reducing or increasing their financial burden.",
          "Avoid partisan abstraction and lead with local delivery: in a suburban district this closely divided, a candidate who campaigns on ideology rather than what they have done or will do to improve schools, manage property taxes, and strengthen local services will lose to an opponent who speaks to material household concerns."
        ]
