          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost for Aging Population", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 42.6 median age, IL-SD-58 is an aging district where healthcare costs, Medicaid access, and Medicare sustainability are direct household concerns for households on fixed or modestly growing incomes. The $64,515 median income provides modest buffer against healthcare shocks, but aging homeowners in this income band face real constraints on out-of-pocket medical expenses and long-term care planning. A legislative record that demonstrates concrete improvement in rural or regional healthcare access, pharmaceutical cost management, or senior financial security is the most credible way to hold this constituency's political allegiance." },
          { name: "Unaccounted Demographic Gap and Latent Organizing Opportunity", tag: "careful", why: "The 3.22% gap in reported demographics (roughly 1,000-1,500 residents) remains unmeasured and could represent an immigrant community, multiracial residents, or other constituencies whose composition and political alignment are unknown. While too small to dominate district character, this population is large enough that if organized by a credible challenger around immigration, civil rights, or cultural integration issues, it could shift a close race. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — should understand this gap's actual composition and address it directly rather than assuming it is politically inert." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Proof of Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed election in IL-SD-58 is not evidence that the district is satisfied with the incumbent or that opposition is impossible; it is evidence that no credible challenger has yet mounted a campaign. The moment a genuine alternative emerges who can articulate specific property tax, education, or healthcare improvements and mobilize the 23.5% renter constituency or the unaccounted demographic gap, the electoral math becomes genuinely uncertain. Incumbents who treat unopposed status as permission to ignore constituency concerns often face shocks when opposition finally materializes." },
          { name: "Overestimating the Stability of a Below-Average College-Attainment, Working-Class Base", tag: "avoid", why: "At 20.1% college attainment — below the state average — IL-SD-58 is a working- and middle-class district whose allegiance is conditioned on material economic delivery, not inherited partisan loyalty or cultural identity. Working-class electorates evaluate representatives on concrete outcomes: lower taxes, better schools, accessible healthcare. A candidate who defaults to ideological positioning or abstract conservative framing, rather than specific answers to the district's actual economic concerns, underestimates both the intelligence of this constituency and the vulnerability that creates for an incumbent who is not delivering." }
